## Series context & the key point _(why this concept, not a generic one)_

Part 3.1 is the **second of two maps of the same territory.** Part 3.0 mapped the body's anabolic machinery by **mechanism** (eight pathways, drawn last run as a mixing console). This article maps the *same compounds* by **lineage** — the chemical family each one descends from. Derek's (More Plates More Dates) framework collapses almost every commonly used anabolic into one of three families, all descending from a single parent:

- **Testosterone family** (Test, Boldenone, Dianabol; + the misfits Halotestin & Turinabol)
- **DHT family** (Masteron, Primobolan, Anavar, Winstrol, Proviron, Superdrol, DHB; + the misfit Anadrol)
- **19-Nortestosterone / Nandrolone family** (Nandrolone "Deca/NPP", Trenbolone, Trestolone "MENT")

The article's controlling line: **"The family predicts the behaviour; the pathway explains it."** Once you know the family, you already know most of what to expect — aromatization, water, progestogenic risk, suppression — before you've looked anything up.

But the **single most clarifying point in the whole article** — its own words — is the **myth-buster**, and it must be the hero of the poster:

> Classic nitrogen-balance studies found the most potent compounds from *every* family retain **roughly the same nitrogen** — on the order of **60–75 g of lean body mass per day** for the strong injectables, with oral 17-alkyls dose-dependent in the same range. The actual contractile tissue you build is **similar across compounds.** What differs is everything *around* it. "The destination (muscle gained) is comparable; the *journey* (water, estrogen, progestogenic effects, neurological feel, organ strain) is what varies wildly."

This is the chemical proof of the entire series rule — **choose by the job, not the hype.** Since raw muscle-building is roughly equivalent, you select a compound for its **side-effect / secondary-mechanism profile**, never for a mythical potency ranking. It is *why* "more / harsher" so rarely beats "moderate / multi-pathway."

Three more load-bearing ideas the visual must carry:

1. **The learning ladder = Test → DHT → 19-nor.** Climb one family at a time, exhaust the cheapest/safest lever before adding the next, never skip to the most suppressive family because it's the most hyped. This is also the *order of derivation* (Test is the root; DHT and Nandrolone both descend from it), so the tree and the ladder are the same shape.
2. **The family is a strong default, not a law — every branch has a misfit.** Halotestin & Turinabol sit in the Test family but **don't aromatize and behave DHT-like.** Anadrol sits in the DHT family but **acts like a 19-nor** (estrogen-receptor agonist, watery mass). MENT sits in the 19-nor family but **aromatizes and behaves Test-like.** The exceptions are teaching objects, not footnotes.
3. **The 19-nor suppression danger.** The 19-nors are the **most suppressive family of the entire tree** — a single nandrolone injection can suppress the HPTA for *months*. Reserve them until you've accepted blast-and-cruise (the Part 1 lifetime-commitment question made chemically concrete). This is the **one true danger** on the poster.

**The single key point to make a reader feel:** _Every family builds about the same muscle. They just charge wildly different rent. So the question is never "which is strongest?" — it's "which side-effect bill am I willing to pay, and have I earned the right to climb to it?"_

This is the concept a generic chat would miss: it would draw a botanical family tree with three branches, or a grid of drug names. The *real* payload is the **shared output datum** — proof the muscle is equal — with the divergence living entirely in the side-effect plume *above* the line, climbed in a fixed order, with misfiled parts flagged and the 19-nor rung carrying the only red.

