## 7. What to Avoid

1. **A botanical/genealogy family tree with three pretty branches and drug names hanging off it.** That is the obvious move and it buries the thesis under decoration. The upgrade is the **shared OUTPUT DATUM**: the tree exists to deliver "all families land on the same muscle line," and the divergence lives in the plumes above it. If the poster doesn't make the equal-floor / unequal-ceiling split, it has missed the article.
2. **A "which is strongest?" ranking — a podium, a bar chart of potency, biggest-to-smallest.** The article exists to *destroy* that question. Any visual that implies a strength ranking between families is the exact opposite of the content. The three families must read as **equal at the muscle line**, differing only in cost.
3. **Treating the misfits as footnotes, or all families in one flat colour.** Halo/Turinabol/Anadrol/MENT being "filed in the wrong drawer" is a core teaching beat — they must visibly sit in one lane while wearing another family's tag, with a cross-link. Drop them and the poster says "the family is a law," which the article explicitly denies.
4. **Flooding it with red, or putting doses on the named anabolics.** Red is one object only (the 19-nor suppression crown); the progestogenic/misfit warnings are amber. And no named anabolic carries a dose anywhere on the poster — house rule, harm-reduction framing only.
